  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 AM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Isolated strong to severe storms, capable of \ndamaging straight-line winds and torrential rainfall will be \npossible Monday and Tuesday.\n\nA weak wave of low pressure is expected to ripple along stationary \nfront draped across the Mid-MS Valley into the Ohio Valley today and \neventually off the NJ coast by Mon morning. The departure of the \nsurface low and strong high pressure sliding eastward into southern \nOntario/Quebec will combine to push the leading edge of a cold front \nthrough VA by Mon morning. South of this boundary, a hot and humid \nair mass will be in place as surface temperatures rise into the \nupper 80s to low 90s with peak heating surface dew points in the \nupper 60s to around 70. This should result in a 1500-2500 J/kg of \nMLCAPE to develop, greatest along and just south of the location of \nthe surface boundary, and support deep convection to develop in the \nvicinity of the surface convergence Mon afternoon.\n\nModerate instability, PWAT values above the 90th percentile per \nGSO/MHX RAOB climatology, and steepening low-lvl lapse rates > 8.5 \nC/km will support water-loaded downbursts bringing a primary concern \nfor damaging straight-line winds. Weak deep-layer wind shear will \nlimit storm organization/longevity providing mainly an isolated \nsevere wind threat; although if updrafts can organize around a \ncommon cold-pool, more scattered instances of strong winds may be \npossible (40-60 mph). Slow storm motions, well-above normal PWAT, \nand a warm cloud-layer around 10 kft will also support efficient \nrainfall producing storms which may cause localized urban flooding \nconcerns.\n\nThe surface cold front will likely continue to slowly ooze south, \nassisted by convective outflow from Mon afternoon/evening \nconvection, and provide a very similar setup for Tues but closer to \nthe NC/SC border. Although showers/storms will still be possible \nnorth of the boundary, limited and elevated instability should \nreduce the threat for both severe and flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Classic cold-air damming pattern will likely \ndevelop by mid-week and bring well-below normal temperatures into \nthe weekend.\n\nCentral NC will likely be on the southern periphery of the more \nclassic CAD setup with the Rex-Blocking configuration over the Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley as opposed to into the TN Valley and \nSoutheast. Additionally, although the surface high pressure is \nstrong (>1030 mb), it is a bit far north (closer to 50 N latitude) \nand elongated east-west to provide a CAD onset from the surface high \nalone. This configuration will likely greatly depend on a surface \nlow to develop and deepen over the western Atlantic to pull the \nfront equatorward as well as low-predictability convectively induced \nMCVs from upstream convection to provide the diabatic component to \nlock in the CAD on Tues to persist into Wed and Thurs; especially \nwith the lack of any low-lvl WAA over NC.\n\nFrom a temperature standpoint, ensemble guidance does support the \nonset of a CAD regime over the area with surface riding extending \ndown through the Carolinas and persistent northerly flow in its wake \nand providing well below-normal temperatures to the region. The \nuncertainty comes from the precipitation amounts we will produce \nfrom this pattern with much of the synoptic forcing remaining in the \nVirginias before transferring to the surface low deepening off the \nMid-Atlantic coast, as well as its proximity to the coast when the \nlow does develop.",
